// A STATED LIMIT, kept executable: the capability model is outside the
// emitted-C comparison entirely (register #9). The port is a strict superset of
// the language the frozen reference can judge, so `rune` / `ward` / `sigil` and
// `seal` have no differential semantics at all.
//
// That is a limit on ONE finding class, not on the fuzzer. These constructs stay
// in the generator's vocabulary and are hunted with the classes that need no
// second opinion - the port's own crashes, its emissions failing the C compiler,
// and its time and memory budgets. Turning the limit into a blanket exclusion
// would write a per-input fact into the contract, and the era's own defects
// lived exactly here. The gate therefore checks BOTH halves: the byte comparison
// blocked, and the port-side classes still live on the same input.
fn main() {
    seal {
        println(1);
    }
    println(2);
}
